  "body": "## Summary\n\nQueue task **6722** (`repo-health-code-pilot`, issue #1348) is bound to session `ses_e5e60bd3d74ffewwDg1Qd0n2Qa`, and that session is alive but has **zero messages**. No rule in the claimant contract can unstick it:\n\n- it is not a launch candidate (it carries `progress.session_id`, and `poll.py::is_candidate` excludes bound tasks);\n- the one permitted prompt resend was already spent by an earlier fire (`progress.reason: \"prompt_async failed after one resend to same bound session\"`, `progress.error_type: \"HTTPError\"`);\n- the expired-lease relaunch rule in step 1 applies only when the session is **gone** — this one answers `GET /api/session/<id>` with 200.\n\nState observed 2026-11-03T04:43Z:\n\n| field | value |\n| --- | --- |\n| status | `claimed` |\n| stage | `handed_off` |\n| attempts | 3 / 3 |\n| lease_until | 2026-11-03T04:43:41Z (expired) |\n| session messages | 0 |\n| session markers | no `time.idle`, no `outcome`, `created == updated` (2026-11-03T04:35:14Z) |\n\n## Impact\n\nThe task occupies a claimed slot forever: no worker, no terminal state, and no path back to the candidate list. Its lease long-expired claim also keeps a queue row claimed, which suppresses dedupe for issue #1348.\n\n## Proposed fix\n\nAdd a recovery rule for the \"created, never prompted, never active\" case, distinct from the gone-session rule. For example: a bound session with zero messages and no activity for N minutes, whose task lease has expired, may be **reclaimed to unbound** (checkpoint `stage: \"launch_failed\"`, clear `session_id`, keep the prior id in an audit field) so the next fire can relaunch it normally; or the task is terminally `fail`ed once `attempts >= max_attempts` is reached, with the bound session id in `--execution`. Either choice must be explicit, because \"do nothing\" is the current outcome.\n\nWhichever rule wins, it should also tell Tend's fleet-watchdog (ledger backstop) what it did, since the watchdog sees the same stuck row.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- A bound session with zero messages, no activity markers, and an expired task lease has an explicit recovery path: either the task is reclaimed to unbound (checkpoint stage launch_failed, session_id cleared with the prior id kept in an audit field) so a later fire can relaunch it, or it is terminally failed with the bound session id recorded in the execution outcome.\n- The chosen rule is written into the claimant contract/instructions, including the inactivity threshold N.\n- Tend's fleet-watchdog (ledger backstop) is told what the rule did (or can read it from the task's progress), so both supervisors agree on the row's state.\n- A live, progressing bound session is never reclaimed or relaunched over (existing hard rule preserved).\n",
